---
import LegalDoc from "../components/LegalDoc.astro";
import InfoLayout from "../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";

const sections = [
    ["who-we-are", "Who we are"],
    ["what-we-collect", "What we collect"],
    ["visits", "Page visits and counts"],
    ["cookies", "Cookies and browser storage"],
    ["google-fonts", "Google Fonts"],
    ["public", "What is public"],
    ["why", "Why we use information"],
    ["providers", "Who handles it for us"],
    ["transfers", "International transfers"],
    ["retention", "Retention and deletion"],
    ["rights", "Your rights"],
    ["children", "Age limit"],
    ["security", "Security"],
    ["changes", "Changes to this policy"],
    ["contact", "Contact"],
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout
    title="Privacy policy"
    description="What Tondo collects, why, who handles it, how long it stays and the rights you have."
>
    <LegalDoc effective="November 1, 2026" sections={sections}>
        <p>
            This policy explains what happens to personal information when you visit tondo.page,
            create an account, build a page or look at someone else's page. Tondo doesn't use
            advertising pixels or analytics cookies, and it doesn't sell personal information.
        </p>
        <p>
            Tondo is in early development and hasn't opened to the public. This policy describes the
            service as it's built to run in production, and it says so where something is planned.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
        <p>
            Tondo is operated by GalleoApp, Inc., a Delaware corporation, referred to here as "we"
            or "us". Our founder works from Vancouver, British Columbia. For the information
            described in this policy, GalleoApp, Inc. is the organization responsible, which is
            called the controller or business in some privacy laws.
        </p>
        <p>
            The content of a Tondo page is chosen by the person who publishes it. If you've been
            named or pictured on a page, you can ask that person to change it or{" "}
            <a href="/report">report the page</a> to us.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-we-collect">What we collect</h2>
        <h3>Account information</h3>
        <p>
            Sign-in runs on Neon Auth, a service from our database provider, Neon. If you sign up
            with an email address, Neon Auth keeps your email address, your sign-in records and your
            password, and we use the part of your email before the @ as your starting name. Your
            password passes through our server on its way to Neon Auth. We don't write it to our own
            tables or to our logs.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you continue with Google, Google tells Neon Auth who you are. That includes your
            email address and name, and it can include your profile picture. We use your email
            address and name to run your account.
        </p>
        <h3>Your page</h3>
        <p>
            We store the address you claim, such as tondo.page/maya, along with your draft and your
            published page. A page holds whatever you add to it, such as your name, a description,
            links and contact details. Don't add anything you wouldn't want to be public to a page
            you plan to publish.
        </p>
        <h3>Photos</h3>
        <p>
            When you upload a photo, our server resizes it and converts it to a WebP file. The
            conversion drops data embedded in the original, such as location data, and only the
            converted file is stored. Each file is stored in a folder named after a hash of your
            user ID.
        </p>
        <h3>Help me</h3>
        <p>
            Help me is an optional assistant in the builder. If you use it, we send your message,
            the text of your draft page and anything you paste or ask it to read to Google, which
            runs the AI model through its Gemini API. We don't send your email address or your
            statistics. If you ask it to read your website, our server fetches that page and sends
            its text to Google. If you ask for a generated image, your description goes to Google.
            If you ask for a stock photo, your search words go to Pexels or Openverse, and the photo
            you pick is copied to your account. If you don't use Help me, none of this happens.
        </p>
        <p>
            Your conversation stays in that browser tab and is gone when you close it. We keep no
            copy of it on our servers. We count how many credits you've used each month, and not
            what you asked. Visitors to your page are never involved.
        </p>
        <h3>Messages you send us</h3>
        <p>
            If you email us, report a page or send a copyright complaint, we keep your email address
            and whatever you wrote, including the details of the page involved. The report link on a
            page opens your own email app, with that page's address in the subject line. Our
            application doesn't record that you pressed it.
        </p>
        <h3>Email we send you</h3>
        <p>
            We use your email address to send account messages, such as verification codes and
            password reset codes. We don't send marketing email today, and there's no mailing list
            in the product.
        </p>

        <h2 id="visits">Page visits and counts</h2>
        <p>
            Tondo counts views of each live page and clicks on its links, so its owner can see how
            the page is doing. Our counts table holds only the page, the link, the day and a running
            total. It doesn't store IP addresses, user agents, referrers, locations, cookies or
            anything else that could tell one visitor from another. The owner sees totals, and can't
            see who visited.
        </p>
        <p>
            To leave crawlers and link previews out of the totals, our server reads the browser's
            user agent as each request arrives, and doesn't keep it. A click on a counted link goes
            through tondo.page, is added to the total and is then sent to its destination. The site
            you land on sees your IP address as it would with any visit, and it usually sees that
            you came from tondo.page.
        </p>
        <h3>IP addresses and rate limits</h3>
        <p>
            Your IP address reaches our server with every request. We use it to limit how many
            requests one address can make, which protects the service against abuse. We use email
            addresses the same way on sign-in, sign-up and code requests, to slow down password
            guessing and to stop anyone flooding an inbox.
        </p>
        <p>
            These limits are held in the server's memory and aren't written to our database. Each
            count applies for a window of one minute or, for some sign-in actions, 15 minutes. An
            expired entry stays in memory until the list fills up and it's pushed out, or until the
            server restarts, which also happens at every deploy.
        </p>
        <h3>Logs</h3>
        <p>
            Our application doesn't write a log line for each visit. It writes error messages to the
            logs that Render keeps. Our hosting and network providers may keep their own connection
            and security logs, including IP addresses, under their own terms.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and browser storage</h2>
        <p>
            Tondo sets cookies only for signing in. These are first-party session cookies from Neon
            Auth, set on our own domain and marked Secure. Someone who only visits a Tondo page
            isn't given a cookie by us, and we use no tracking or advertising cookies. Live pages
            tell the browser to run no scripts at all.
        </p>
        <p>The editor and sign-in pages also keep a few things in your browser:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>A copy of unsaved work.</strong> The editor keeps a copy of your page in
                local storage until the server has it, so a lost connection or a closed tab doesn't
                lose your changes. The copy normally clears after a successful save.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A tab ID.</strong> A random ID in session storage keeps each tab's unsaved
                work apart.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your email address, briefly.</strong> During verification or a password
                reset, session storage holds your email address and the address you asked for, for
                that tab only.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            This data stays in your browser and isn't sent to anyone unless it's saved to your
            account. Logging out or deleting your account doesn't clear an unsaved copy from local
            storage. If you use a shared computer, clear this site's data in your browser settings.
        </p>

        <h2 id="google-fonts">Google Fonts</h2>
        <p>
            Our website, the editor and every live Tondo page load their typefaces from Google
            Fonts. When a page opens, the visitor's browser connects to fonts.googleapis.com and
            fonts.gstatic.com. That sends Google the visitor's IP address and browser details, along
            with the fonts requested and usually the site they were requested from. This happens
            before anyone can make a choice about it, and it applies to people who only visit a
            page.
        </p>
        <p>
            Google handles that information under its own terms and privacy policy, and we don't
            receive it. If you block these requests in your browser, text appears in another
            typeface.
        </p>

        <h2 id="public">What is public</h2>
        <p>
            When you publish, your page is available to anyone at its address, without signing in.
            We don't ask search engines to skip live pages, so they can be indexed. Other people,
            search engines and archives can copy what they find, and we can't recall those copies.
        </p>
        <p>
            A draft isn't public, but your uploaded photos are served from an address that needs no
            sign-in. The address is long and hard to guess, and anyone who has it can open the
            photo, whether or not the photo is on a live page. Please don't upload a photo you need
            to keep private.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every live page has a "Report this page" link, which opens tondo.page/report. Anyone who
            is signed in can also check whether an address is taken.
        </p>

        <h2 id="why">Why we use information</h2>
        <p>
            We use information to run your account and page, keep the service secure, handle
            reports, answer you and meet legal duties. We don't build advertising profiles. Canadian
            privacy law asks us to rely on your consent, which you give when you create an account
            or send us a message, and you can withdraw it by deleting your account or contacting us,
            subject to legal limits. Under the GDPR and UK GDPR, we rely on these lawful bases:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Performing our contract with you:</strong> creating and signing in to your
                account, storing your draft and photos, publishing your page, sending account codes,
                and carrying out what you ask Help me to do.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Legitimate interests:</strong> rate limits and other security measures,
                abuse reports and takedowns, page view and click totals, error logs, and loading
                fonts so pages appear as designed. You can object to these as described below.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Legal obligations:</strong> answering lawful requests, and keeping records
                the law requires.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Consent:</strong> where we ask for it. We don't rely on it for anything
                today.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="providers">Who handles it for us</h2>
        <p>
            We share information with the following companies so they can run parts of the service:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Neon</strong> hosts our Postgres database and Neon Auth, which handles
                sign-in, passwords and sessions.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Render</strong> hosts the server that runs the website, editor and pages.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cloudflare</strong> is planned to store uploaded photos in its R2 storage in
                production. Photos are delivered through our server, so loading one doesn't connect
                a visitor to Cloudflare.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Google</strong> provides Google sign-in if you choose it, and Google Fonts
                for everyone.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Google's Gemini API</strong> runs Help me, if you use it. It receives your
                messages, your draft's text, text from a website you ask it to read, and image
                descriptions. Tondo uses the paid service, under which Google says it doesn't use
                prompts or responses to improve its products, and logs them for a limited period to
                detect and prevent policy violations and for legal reasons.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Pexels and Openverse</strong> receive your search words if you ask Help me
                for a stock photo.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Email delivery:</strong> Neon Auth sends account emails, through Neon's own
                sender or an email provider we connect to it. We'll name any separate provider here
                when we add one.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            We may also disclose information when the law requires it, to protect people or the
            service from harm, or as part of a sale or reorganization of our business. We don't sell
            personal information or share it for cross-context behavioural advertising.
        </p>

        <h2 id="transfers">International transfers</h2>
        <p>
            Tondo's database is set up in Neon's AWS us-east-2 region, and its server in Render's
            Ohio region, both in the United States. Google and Cloudflare can process data in other
            countries, including what Help me sends to Google, and our founder can access it from
            Canada. If you use Tondo from elsewhere, your information will leave your country, and
            it can be available to courts and authorities in the countries where it's held.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where the GDPR or UK GDPR requires a safeguard for a transfer, we rely on a mechanism
            recognized by that law, such as standard contractual clauses in a provider's data
            processing terms. You can ask us about these at{" "}
            <a href="mailto:privacy@tondo.page">privacy@tondo.page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="retention">Retention and deletion</h2>
        <h3>Unpublishing</h3>
        <p>
            Unpublishing takes your page off its address straight away. Your draft, address, photos,
            counts and account all stay, so you can publish again. Copies made by others, such as
            search engine results, aren't affected.
        </p>
        <h3>Deleting your account</h3>
        <p>
            You can delete your account from the Account page. That removes your uploaded photos,
            pages, counts and address from our live systems, and asks Neon Auth to delete your
            sign-in record. If Neon Auth can't complete that step, we tell you, and you can email{" "}
            <a href="mailto:privacy@tondo.page">privacy@tondo.page</a> to have it removed.
        </p>
        <p>Some things can remain after that:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Backups.</strong> Neon keeps a rolling restore history for a limited period,
                and deleted data stays there until that period passes. Other providers may also take
                a short time to clear deleted data from their systems.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cached photos.</strong> Photos are sent with a one-year browser cache
                setting, so a browser that has already loaded one may keep showing its copy.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Pages taken down for abuse.</strong> If your page was suspended, its address
                and the suspension date stay on record, without your user ID, so that the address
                stays out of use and the page can't return under a new account.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Messages.</strong> Reports, complaints and other emails are kept as
                described below.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Browser copies.</strong> Any unsaved copy stays in that browser until
                cleared.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>How long we keep things</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Account information, your page, your photos and your counts stay until you delete
                them or your account. We don't currently delete inactive accounts automatically, and
                counts don't expire on a schedule.
            </li>
            <li>Rate limit entries stay only in server memory, as described above.</li>
            <li>
                We keep emails, reports and complaints for as long as we need them to handle the
                matter, keep a record of decisions about abuse, and meet legal duties.
            </li>
            <li>
                Credit counts stay until you delete your account. What Help me sends to Google stays
                with Google for the limited period described above, and we can't delete it there.
                Generated images you didn't pick are removed when you close their card, and ones you
                keep are stored like your other photos.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
        <p>
            You can see and change most of your information in your account, and delete it there.
            For anything else, email <a href="mailto:privacy@tondo.page">privacy@tondo.page</a>. We
            may need to confirm your identity first, and we'll answer within the time the applicable
            law allows. We won't treat you differently for exercising a right. The rights you have
            depend on where you live.
        </p>
        <h3>Canada</h3>
        <p>
            Where PIPEDA or British Columbia's Personal Information Protection Act (PIPA) applies,
            you can ask for access to your personal information and how it has been used, ask for
            corrections, and withdraw consent, subject to legal limits. If you're not satisfied with
            our answer, you can complain to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada or the
            Office of the Information and Privacy Commissioner for British Columbia.
        </p>
        <h3>European Economic Area and United Kingdom</h3>
        <p>
            Under the GDPR and UK GDPR, you can ask for access, correction, deletion, restriction
            and portability of your personal data. You can object to processing based on legitimate
            interests, and withdraw consent where we rely on it. You can complain to your local data
            protection authority, or in the UK to the Information Commissioner's Office.
        </p>
        <h3>California</h3>
        <p>
            Where the CCPA applies, you can ask to know what personal information we collect, use
            and disclose, and ask us to correct or delete it. You can use an authorized agent, and
            we won't discriminate against you for making a request. In the categories that law uses,
            we handle identifiers (email address, IP address), account log-in details, internet
            activity (requests to our server), and the content you add. We use log-in details only
            to sign you in. We don't sell personal information or share it for cross-context
            behavioural advertising. Since we don't do either, there's nothing for a Global Privacy
            Control or Do Not Track signal to switch off, and those signals don't change how Tondo
            behaves.
        </p>

        <h2 id="children">Age limit</h2>
        <p>
            You must be at least 18 to create a Tondo account. We don't knowingly collect personal
            information from anyone younger. Sign-up doesn't verify age, so if you think someone
            under 18 has given us information, please email{" "}
            <a href="mailto:privacy@tondo.page">privacy@tondo.page</a> and we'll look into it and
            remove it where appropriate.
        </p>

        <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
        <p>
            Tondo is served over HTTPS only. Each signed-in request acts only on your own page,
            sign-in attempts are rate limited, and live pages run no scripts. Uploaded images are
            checked and converted before they're stored. No service can promise complete security,
            so please use a strong, unique password. If a breach affects you and the law requires us
            to tell you, we will.
        </p>

        <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
            We'll update this page when what we do changes, and change the effective date at the
            top. If a change materially affects you, we'll also tell you by email or in the product
            before it takes effect. Features such as forms, payments, connected accounts or optional
            analytics aren't built. If we add them, we'll update this policy first.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>
            For privacy questions and requests, including to reach the person responsible for
            privacy at GalleoApp, Inc., email{" "}
            <a href="mailto:privacy@tondo.page">privacy@tondo.page</a>. To report a page, use{" "}
            <a href="mailto:report@tondo.page">report@tondo.page</a>. For other legal matters, see
            our <a href="/terms">terms of service</a> or email{" "}
            <a href="mailto:legal@tondo.page">legal@tondo.page</a>.
        </p>
    </LegalDoc>
</InfoLayout>
